WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.292
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Proficiency.

2
00:00:05.592 --> 00:00:08.549
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.849 --> 00:00:17.086
Unit fifty-four: Fiction, Truth and Empathy. In this unit, you will argue whether fiction can make us better people.

4
00:00:18.083 --> 00:00:19.526
narrative empathy.

5
00:00:20.226 --> 00:00:25.174
narrative empathy means feeling with characters as we read or watch their stories.

6
00:00:25.474 --> 00:00:28.728
Narrative empathy lets us share a stranger's grief.

7
00:00:29.428 --> 00:00:32.428
Your turn. Say: narrative empathy.

8
00:00:35.128 --> 00:00:36.431
narrative empathy.

9
00:00:37.417 --> 00:00:38.638
fictional truth.

10
00:00:39.338 --> 00:00:44.577
fictional truth means a deep truth about life expressed through invented stories.

11
00:00:44.877 --> 00:00:48.026
Fictional truth can be more honest than a news report.

12
00:00:48.726 --> 00:00:52.052
Your turn. Say: fictional truth.

13
00:00:54.752 --> 00:00:56.240
fictional truth.

14
00:00:57.250 --> 00:00:58.750
moral imagination.

15
00:00:59.450 --> 00:01:04.306
moral imagination means the ability to imagine how our actions affect others.

16
00:01:04.606 --> 00:01:07.395
Literature develops our moral imagination.

17
00:01:08.095 --> 00:01:11.154
Your turn. Say: moral imagination.

18
00:01:13.854 --> 00:01:15.284
moral imagination.

19
00:01:16.283 --> 00:01:17.772
literary realism.

20
00:01:18.472 --> 00:01:24.558
literary realism means writing that represents ordinary life truthfully and in detail.

21
00:01:24.858 --> 00:01:28.530
Literary realism shows poverty without romance.

22
00:01:29.230 --> 00:01:32.648
Your turn. Say: literary realism.

23
00:01:35.348 --> 00:01:36.756
literary realism.

24
00:01:37.750 --> 00:01:39.030
perspective-taking.

25
00:01:39.730 --> 00:01:45.328
perspective-taking means imagining a situation from another person's point of view.

26
00:01:45.628 --> 00:01:48.766
Debate practice improves perspective-taking.

27
00:01:49.466 --> 00:01:52.896
Your turn. Say: perspective-taking.

28
00:01:55.596 --> 00:01:56.806
perspective-taking.

29
00:01:57.817 --> 00:01:59.549
suspension of disbelief.

30
00:02:00.249 --> 00:02:05.767
suspension of disbelief means temporarily accepting impossible things in a story.

31
00:02:06.067 --> 00:02:09.274
Fantasy requires a willing suspension of disbelief.

32
00:02:09.974 --> 00:02:13.729
Your turn. Say: suspension of disbelief.

33
00:02:16.429 --> 00:02:18.138
suspension of disbelief.

34
00:02:19.150 --> 00:02:20.348
character arc.

35
00:02:21.048 --> 00:02:25.277
character arc means the way a character changes during a story.

36
00:02:25.577 --> 00:02:29.585
The hero's character arc moves from selfishness to sacrifice.

37
00:02:30.285 --> 00:02:33.402
Your turn. Say: character arc.

38
00:02:36.102 --> 00:02:37.254
character arc.

39
00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:39.669
narrative ethics.

40
00:02:40.369 --> 00:02:45.666
narrative ethics means the study of the moral questions raised by telling stories.

41
00:02:45.966 --> 00:02:50.380
Narrative ethics asks whose story is being told, and by whom.

42
00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:54.615
Your turn. Say: narrative ethics.

43
00:02:57.315 --> 00:02:58.908
narrative ethics.

44
00:02:59.917 --> 00:03:02.148
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:03:02.448 --> 00:03:05.087
Rithy, does reading novels make us kinder?

46
00:03:06.383 --> 00:03:10.136
Some studies suggest so, although the evidence is mixed.

47
00:03:11.450 --> 00:03:12.741
Mixed in what way?

48
00:03:14.050 --> 00:03:19.939
While readers show more perspective-taking, it's unclear whether they act more kindly.

49
00:03:21.250 --> 00:03:23.911
So narrative empathy may stay on the page.

50
00:03:25.217 --> 00:03:30.934
Possibly. Even so, a novel can open our moral imagination to lives we'd never meet.

51
00:03:32.250 --> 00:03:34.737
Yet fiction is, after all, invented.

52
00:03:36.050 --> 00:03:42.499
Invented, yes; untrue, no. Fictional truth can reveal what facts alone cannot.

53
00:03:43.783 --> 00:03:47.408
Grammar focus: Concession and contrast (advanced).

54
00:03:47.708 --> 00:03:49.952
Concession makes arguments nuanced.

55
00:03:50.252 --> 00:03:55.316
Some studies suggest reading builds empathy, although the evidence is mixed.

56
00:03:55.616 --> 00:03:58.336
Fiction is invented, yes; untrue, no.

57
00:03:58.636 --> 00:04:03.990
'Yes ... no' structures and 'even so' allow elegant concessions in speech and essays alike.

58
00:04:04.983 --> 00:04:08.077
Now read and listen. Can Stories Make Us Better?.

59
00:04:08.377 --> 00:04:12.211
For centuries, writers have claimed that literature educates the heart.

60
00:04:13.517 --> 00:04:19.858
Psychologists have tested this claim, finding that readers of fiction often score higher in perspective-taking.

61
00:04:21.150 --> 00:04:28.003
Narrative empathy, the experience of feeling with a character, may train us to understand real people.

62
00:04:29.317 --> 00:04:35.565
Literary realism, by portraying ordinary suffering in detail, can widen our moral imagination.

63
00:04:36.850 --> 00:04:44.028
Even fantasy, which demands suspension of disbelief, can explore real moral dilemmas through a character arc.

64
00:04:45.317 --> 00:04:51.473
Nevertheless, some researchers caution that feeling empathy for characters does not always lead to kinder action.

65
00:04:52.783 --> 00:04:59.891
Narrative ethics also asks difficult questions: who has the right to tell another community's story?

66
00:05:01.183 --> 00:05:05.958
Fiction may not change the world directly; even so, it can change the people who will.

67
00:05:07.250 --> 00:05:09.786
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:10.086 --> 00:05:14.408
Imagining a situation from another person's viewpoint is blank.

69
00:05:14.708 --> 00:05:20.428
A, perspective-taking. B, character arc. C, literary realism.

70
00:05:24.417 --> 00:05:27.078
The answer is A: perspective-taking.

71
00:05:28.583 --> 00:05:30.583
Which word introduces a concession?

72
00:05:30.883 --> 00:05:35.128
A, although. B, because. C, therefore.

73
00:05:39.117 --> 00:05:41.372
The answer is A: although.

74
00:05:42.883 --> 00:05:46.590
'Invented, yes; untrue, no' means fiction is blank.

75
00:05:46.890 --> 00:05:52.157
A, made up but still truthful. B, false. C, true facts.

76
00:05:56.150 --> 00:05:59.496
The answer is A: made up but still truthful.

77
00:06:00.983 --> 00:06:04.782
Temporarily accepting impossible things in a story is blank.

78
00:06:05.082 --> 00:06:11.290
A, suspension of disbelief. B, narrative ethics. C, fictional truth.

79
00:06:15.283 --> 00:06:17.654
The answer is A: suspension of disbelief.

80
00:06:19.150 --> 00:06:22.787
According to the text, what does narrative ethics ask?

81
00:06:23.087 --> 00:06:30.444
A, who has the right to tell a story. B, how long a novel should be. C, which genre sells best.

82
00:06:34.450 --> 00:06:37.599
The answer is A: who has the right to tell a story.

83
00:06:39.083 --> 00:06:44.464
Now it's your turn. Part one: Can reading fiction make young people more compassionate?

84
00:06:49.764 --> 00:06:54.666
Part two: Concede one limitation, then counter it with an example.

85
00:07:00.966 --> 00:07:13.072
Although reading alone cannot make anyone kind, it helps. While I was reading a novel about refugees, I understood their fear for the first time. Even so, we must turn empathy into action.

86
00:07:13.372 --> 00:07:15.325
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:16.317 --> 00:07:21.755
Great work today! You learned eight new words and concession and contrast (advanced).

88
00:07:22.055 --> 00:07:25.625
Today's value is empathy. Think about how others feel.

89
00:07:25.925 --> 00:07:28.751
See you in Unit fifty-five. Goodbye!

90
00:07:29.051 --> 00:07:32.238
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

